[%in 2] [%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 1308] The knight on g6 attacks f4, but f4
is protected twice, by Bc1 and Rf1. The bigger issue is the long diagonal:
Black can play ...Qb6 to pin my d4 knight against the king, then ...c5 to
attack it. Be3 handles all of this at once. It develops the bishop, protects d4
again, blocks the a7–g1 diagonal and protects f4 once more. Check of possible
replies: - **...Ng4 attacking the bishop:** Bxg4 Bxg4 Qxg4, and Black loses a
piece. - **...Nxe4:** Nxe4, and Black gets only one pawn for the knight. -
**...Nxf4:** Bxf4, which also just wins the knight. Later I can play Qd2 and
Rad1, and push f5 when the timing is right. } 10... c6 11. Bf3 { [%emt 0:07:42]
