[%cachewrite 1661] **Analysis:** Black's f6 attacks my bishop on e5. The bishop
can simply take: **Bxf6**. Taking on f6 wins a pawn and opens the e-file, so my
queen on e4 attacks the bishop on e7. - **25…Bxf6?** 26. Qe8# is mate. f8 and
f7 are covered by the queen, and h8 is on the same line. Black has no piece
that can capture on e8 or block on f8. - **25…gxf6** 26. Qxe7 wins the bishop
back and leaves Black's king open. There are threats like Rg3+ and Qf8#. -
**25…Qc5 (defending e7)** 26. Bxe7. The e7 bishop is also attacking the queen
on c5. If 26…Qxe7, then 27. Qxe7 wins the queen. - **25…Qb4 (attacking my
queen)** 26. Qe6+ Kh8 27. Qxe7 wins a whole piece. - **25…Kf7** 26. Qe6+ leads
quickly to mate (Qxe7# or Qxg7#). - **Black's checks:** - Qf1+ is met by Rxf1.
- Qa4+ is met by Qxa4 along the fourth rank. - Qd3+ is met by Qxd3. - Qd5+ is
met by Qxd5+. - Qxb2 is met by Bxb2, since the bishop on f6 covers the long
diagonal. So Black has no perpetual check. } 25... Bxf6 26. Qe8# {
